<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">French Third Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1870 CE – 1940 CE · capital Paris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's longest-lasting regime since the Revolution, which built secular schools and a colonial empire, won the First World War and fell in 1940.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III's</a> capture at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-sedan">Sedan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> proclaimed the Republic on 4 September 1870. Defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the loss of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imperial-territory-of-alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> and the crushing of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> followed. A monarchist assembly could not agree on a king, and the constitutional laws of 1875 created a parliamentary republic almost by default.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Strong parliaments and short-lived cabinets governed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry's</a> laws made primary schooling free, compulsory and secular, and the 1905 law separated church and state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair">Dreyfus Affair</a> divided the nation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built a large empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>. It won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> at a cost of 1.4 million dead, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-france">Popular Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-blum">Léon Blum</a> introduced paid holidays in 1936.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> invasion in May 1940 brought rapid defeat. On 10 July 1940 parliament voted full powers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a>, ending the Republic and creating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vichy-france">Vichy France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-third-republic.jpg" alt="French Third Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1870 CE – 1940 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolphe Thiers, Georges Clemenceau, Léon Blum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Third Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1905&amp;focus=french-third-republic" aria-label="French Third Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4734 472 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Third Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5054 728L5905 1205M5054 728L5886 1101M5054 728L5509 983M5054 728L5054 728M5054 728L5054 728M5054 728L5054 728M5054 728L5081 810M5054 728L5180 736M5054 728L5180 736"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5905 1205h0M5886 1101h0M5509 983h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M5081 810h0M5180 736h0M5180 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Paris. <a href="/atlas?year=1905&amp;focus=french-third-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
